\begin{abstract}
Large language models are increasingly used as annotators in computational social science, yet annotation errors propagate into downstream causal estimates in ways that depend on both the confusion matrix structure and the causal DAG topology. We derive bias expressions for seven canonical topologies under $K$-class non-differential misclassification and show that $K {\geq} 3$ introduces a structural possibility of sign reversal absent in the binary case. We prove sign preservation for confounding and collider, and verify it numerically for mediation, M-bias, and front-door; exposure and IV topologies can reverse the treatment effect sign. Under realistic LLM accuracy (diagonal ${\geq}\,0.7$), the sign reversal rate is 2.1\%, exclusively in secondary coefficients---across all 23 empirical configurations tested, the primary coefficient does not reverse, an empirical regularity consistent with magnitude dominance in the misclassification-induced weighted sum rather than an algebraic guarantee. We introduce Annotation Sensitivity Values (ASVs) revealing a vulnerability spectrum from IV (maximally fragile) to M-bias (immune), and show that matrix-based corrections are provably ineffective for four topologies due to Frisch--Waugh--Lovell invariance. Topology-aware analysis is necessary for valid causal inference with LLM-generated annotations.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Large language models are rapidly replacing human annotators in computational social science, with researchers using LLM-generated labels for tasks ranging from sentiment classification to political stance detection~\cite{gilardi2023chatgpt, ziems2024can, egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023, baumann2025}. Crucially, most of these tasks are inherently multi-class: stance detection uses 3--4 categories, sentiment analysis 5, and disaster response taxonomies 10 or more. This shift promises dramatic cost reduction, but introduces a critical validity gap: annotation errors from imperfect classifiers propagate into downstream causal estimates in ways that depend on both the error structure and the causal design. \citet{baumann2025} demonstrate that across 37 annotation tasks, 31\% of hypotheses yield incorrect conclusions with state-of-the-art LLMs, rising to 50\% for smaller models; these errors are highly correlated across models (60\% agreement when both err)~\cite{kim_garg2025}, undermining ensemble-based mitigation. Analyzing the \emph{structure} of annotation error, rather than simply reducing its magnitude, is essential for valid downstream inference.

The transition at $K{\geq}3$ is not gradual but structural. For $K{=}2$, the stochastic constraint forces any confusion matrix with positive diagonal to preserve coefficient signs---an algebraic property of the $2{\times}2$ matrix dimension~\cite{brenner1993}. This guarantee \emph{vanishes} at $K{\geq}3$: the higher-dimensional confusion matrix admits negative effective weights on categories with opposite-sign coefficients, enabling sign reversal even under high accuracy~\cite{veirod_laake2001}. Under a uniform Dirichlet prior, 83.4\% of $K{=}3$ exposure confusion matrices produce sign reversal of at least one secondary coefficient; under realistic accuracy (diagonal $\geq 0.7$), 2.1\% still do. The primary treatment coefficient, however, is never reversed in any tested configuration---an empirical regularity we discuss in Section~\ref{sec:method}.

Standard classification metrics do not predict downstream causal risk: within a narrow $F_1$ range of 0.614--0.690 across three frontier LLMs, relative bias varies by an order of magnitude across topologies (from 4.1\% to 42.1\%), illustrating that \emph{topology}, not annotator quality, determines downstream risk. This fundamental asymmetry has received limited systematic treatment. \citet{dosemeci1990} and \citet{veirod_laake2001} noted the possibility of polytomous sign reversal; \citet{brenner1993} identified amplification for polytomous confounders; \citet{lee_wood_doughty2024} extended classifier error analysis to categorical variables and LLM classifiers, but covered only one DAG topology. No prior work systematically characterizes how the \emph{same} confusion matrix produces qualitatively different bias across causal designs. Our analytical predictions match Monte Carlo estimates within 1.6\% MAPE across 161 scenarios.

We address these gaps with three contributions:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Multi-class bias taxonomy.} We derive bias expressions for seven canonical DAG topologies (Figure~\ref{fig:dag_taxonomy}) under $K$-class non-differential misclassification in linear structural equation models, prove sign preservation for confounding and collider, verify it numerically for mediation, M-bias, and front-door, and show that exposure and IV can produce sign reversal. Binarization amplifies the gap: collapsing $K{=}3$ to binary introduces additional bias varying 154$\times$ across topologies.\footnote{Computed as the ratio of maximum to minimum binarization-induced bias across seven topologies under $K{=}3$ Dirichlet$(1,1,1)$ confusion matrices; see Appendix~\ref{app:binarization}.}

\item \textbf{Annotation Sensitivity Values.} As a corollary of the bias taxonomy, we define three complementary ASV types---magnitude, significance, and sign-flip---revealing a topology-dependent vulnerability spectrum from IV (maximally fragile, 52.6\% sign reversal) to M-bias (immune, ASV $= \infty$).

\item \textbf{Topology-aware correction guidance.} We evaluate five correction methods and prove a Frisch--Waugh--Lovell invariance result: matrix-based corrections achieve exactly 0\% bias reduction for four topologies where $A$ enters as a covariate---a negative result overlooked in the annotation correction literature. PPI++ achieves 47.0\% RMSE reduction but requires gold labels and is harmful for M-bias ($-$88\%).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We validate through Monte Carlo simulation ($K{=}3$), cross-$K$ robustness checks ($K{=}5,7$), and empirical validation with real LLM confusion matrices from three NLP tasks ($K{=}2,3,10$).

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

For each of the seven topologies (confounding, exposure, mediation, collider, M-bias, front-door, IV), we sample 100{,}000 confusion matrices from a Dirichlet$(1,1,1)$ prior, compute the analytical probability limit of the causal estimator, and validate against Monte Carlo simulation ($N{=}10{,}000$). ASV experiments use $C(\delta) = (1{-}\delta)I + \delta C_0$.

\subsection{Bias Taxonomy}
\label{sec:taxonomy_results}

\paragraph{Theory-MC agreement.}
Analytical probability limits match Monte Carlo estimates with grand MAPE of 1.6\% across 161 scenarios (23 LLM configurations $\times$ 7 topologies, $N{=}10{,}000$). Per-topology MAPE ranges from 0.3\% (confounding) to 3.7\% (IV); the IV maximum reflects the Wald estimator's small-denominator variance amplification.

\paragraph{Exposure DAG: sign reversal under multi-class misclassification.}
Under realistic LLM accuracy (diagonal $\geq 0.7$), the violation rate is 13.7\% with 2.1\% sign reversal; even at diagonal $\geq 0.9$, violation persists at 11.9\%. Under the full Dirichlet$(1,1,1)$ prior, 85\% of $K{=}3$ confusion matrices violate the binary ``toward-null'' guarantee, and 83.4\% cause sign reversal of at least one coefficient. The $K{=}2$ control confirms that all tested binary matrices maintain classical attenuation~\cite{brenner1993}, establishing that the multi-class failure is structurally distinct.

\paragraph{Structural guarantee for non-exposure topologies.}
Five of seven topologies exhibit zero sign-flip rate across all sampled matrices (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}). Confounding, mediation, and front-door show 100\% amplification~\cite{brenner1993, dosemeci1990} but never reverse the sign. Collider misclassification is protective ($|B(C)|$ approximately non-increasing; Conjecture~\ref{conj:monotone}). M-bias shows the weakest sensitivity (53.2\% violation, 10.4\% amplification).

\paragraph{IV: Wald estimator instability.}
The IV topology is the sole non-exposure exception: 52.6\% of random matrices cause the Wald estimator's denominator to change sign. This rate is stable across $K{=}3,5,7$, confirming structural fragility. Unlike exposure, IV violation remains at 100\% even at diagonal $\geq 0.9$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:iv_dirichlet_subspace}).

All structural patterns persist at $K{=}5,7$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:cross_k_robustness}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Bias under $K{=}3$ non-differential misclassification (100{,}000 Dirichlet$(1,1,1)$ matrices). $\tau_{\text{sf}}{=}0$?: sign-flip guarantee holds unconditionally (\checkmark), conditionally (\checkmark$^*$; Proposition~\ref{prop:signflip}), or not (\ding{55}).}
\label{tab:taxonomy}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Topology} & \textbf{Violation} & \textbf{Sign Flip} & \textbf{Amplif.} & $\tau_{\text{sf}}{=}0$? \\
\midrule
Confounding & 100\% & 0\% & 100\% & \checkmark$^*$ \\
Exposure & 85\% & 83.4\% & 14.1\% & \ding{55} \\
Mediation & 100\% & 0\% & 100\% & \checkmark$^*$ \\
Front-door & 100\% & 0\% & 100\% & \checkmark$^*$ \\
Collider & 100\% & 0\% & 100\% & \checkmark$^*$ \\
M-bias & 53.2\% & 0\% & 10.4\% & \checkmark \\
IV & 100\% & 52.6\% & 100\% & \ding{55} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The bias distributions are visualized in Figure~\ref{fig:bias_distribution}.

\subsection{ASV Framework Validation}
\label{sec:asv_results}

\paragraph{Envelope monotonicity and magnitude ASV.}
The ASV envelope is $\varepsilon$-approximately monotonic for non-IV topologies ($\varepsilon < 0.06$; Conjecture~\ref{conj:monotone}), confirming $\delta$ as a meaningful severity dial. IV is excluded due to first-stage singularities.
Table~\ref{tab:asv_grid} reports magnitude ASV ranges at $\tau{=}0.5$ with a 10\% bias threshold, ranking from most fragile to most robust: front-door (0.064--0.387) $<$ mediation $<$ confounding $<$ IV (0.101--0.607) $<$ collider (0.338--2.028) $<$ M-bias ($\infty$). M-bias achieves infinite ASV because annotation error only reduces the already-small spurious path.

\paragraph{Significance and sign-flip ASV.}
At $\tau{=}0.05$, significance ASV achieves 100\% power for confounding, front-door, and M-bias, but 0\% for mediation, collider, and IV (correct behavior for attenuating topologies; Type~I error is 0\%). For sign-flip ASV, 83.4\% of exposure reference matrices yield $\tau_{\text{sign\_flip}} < 1$ (extreme sensitivity); IV sign-flip ASV ranges 1.1--6.7 (moderate-to-high). The complete ranking (Figure~\ref{fig:asv_ranking}, Table~\ref{tab:asv_grid}, Appendix~\ref{app:supplementary}) confirms IV as maximally fragile and M-bias as immune (ASV $= \infty$).

\paragraph{$N$-sensitivity.}
Significance ASV exhibits a sharp phase transition at a critical $N^* \approx 6{,}000$--$10{,}000$ (analytically computable per topology), below which all rejections are blocked. Bootstrap CIs ($B{=}1{,}000$; Appendix~\ref{sec:bootstrap_asv}): $\delta^* = 0.94\,[0.60, 1.36]$ (exposure), $0.75\,[0.44, 1.29]$ (IV). The wide IV interval reflects the Wald estimator's denominator instability; point estimates should be interpreted with caution when the first stage is weak. Despite CI width, the topology ranking (IV most fragile, M-bias immune) is stable across all bootstrap replicates.

\subsection{Empirical Validation}
\label{sec:empirical}

We validate using real LLM confusion matrices from three tasks: HumAID~\cite{alam2021humaid} ($K{=}10$), VAST ($K{=}3$), and CivilComments ($K{=}2$). Four LLMs (Llama-4-Maverick, Qwen3-235B, DeepSeek-Chat, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) produce 23 configurations across the three datasets under zero-shot and few-shot prompting, yielding 161 bias scenarios ($23 \times 7$ topologies).

\paragraph{Empirical bias ranking.}
HumAID ($K{=}10$) matches predictions: IV (12.4--42.1\%), exposure (9.0--17.4\%), mediation/front-door (10.7--14.8\%), collider/confounding/M-bias $<$10\%. Bias varies substantially across $K$: VAST ($K{=}3$) produces 62.8\% mean bias with IV reaching 326\%, versus 12.2\% for HumAID. Even at $F_1{=}0.82$, IV bias reaches 62--92\%, confirming that classification accuracy does not predict causal risk (Figure~\ref{fig:f1_vs_bias}).

Checking all $K{-}1$ exposure coefficients, 14/23 configurations (61\%) exhibit sign reversal---all in secondary coefficients with small $|\beta|$ (Numerical Verification~2); zero reversals occur across 138 non-exposure scenarios ($23 {\times} 6$ topologies). ASV diagnostics flag 55.4\% of HumAID and 100\% of CivilComments as magnitude-unsafe (Appendix~\ref{sec:asv_diagnostics}); sign-flip patterns persist under logistic DGP ($|\Delta\,\text{SF}_\tau| < 0.02$; Appendix~\ref{sec:logistic_dgp}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig5_f1_vs_bias.pdf}
\caption{Topology determines downstream causal bias, not $F_1$. HumAID $K{=}10$ (56 points): within $F_1$ range 0.614--0.690, bias varies $10{\times}$ (IV 42.1\% vs.\ collider $<$5.6\%).}
\label{fig:f1_vs_bias}
\end{figure}


\subsection{Correction Guidance}
\label{sec:correction}

We evaluate five correction methods (naive plug-in, MLA, DSL~\cite{egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023}, MC-SIMEX, PPI++~\cite{angelopoulos2023}) across all seven topologies under six error structures (210 configurations; full results in Table~\ref{tab:correction}, Appendix~\ref{app:supplementary}).

\paragraph{FWL invariance.}
Under linear OLS, for four topologies where $A$ enters as a covariate, FWL implies that any linear transformation of $\mathbf{D}^*$ preserves the column space relative to $T$, leaving the treatment coefficient unchanged: naive plug-in and MLA achieve exactly 0.0\% bias reduction (Appendix~\ref{app:fwl_proof}). This result is specific to linear models; under logistic regression the invariance breaks, but empirical breakdown is negligible ($< 0.1\%$; Appendix~\ref{sec:fwl_logistic}).

\paragraph{Method ranking.}
PPI++ achieves the highest grand-mean RMSE reduction (47.0\%) but requires gold labels and is harmful for M-bias ($-$88\%). MC-SIMEX provides gold-free correction (best for IV at 59.4\%) but harms front-door ($-$62.4\%). For protective topologies, correction re-exposes the original bias pathway. No single method dominates: correction effectiveness is itself topology-dependent (Appendix~\ref{app:case_study}).

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Measurement error in causal inference.}
The foundations are established in \citet{carroll2006}, \citet{gustafson2004}, \citet{buonaccorsi2010}, and \citet{bound2001measurement}, with starred-node DAGs formalized by \citet{hernan_cole2009} and DAG-based correction theory developed by \citet{kuroki_pearl2014}. For binary variables, non-differential misclassification biases toward the null~\cite{brenner1993, ogburn_vanderweele2012}, with \citet{neuhaus1999} quantifying the attenuation and efficiency loss in binary regression under known misclassification rates; but this fails for polytomous exposures~\cite{dosemeci1990, jurek2005, veirod_laake2001}. \citet{edwards_lessler_naimi2021} showed dependent misclassification can bias in either direction, and \citet{shu_yi2019a, shu_yi2019b} develop weighted estimators that jointly handle mismeasured covariates and misclassified outcomes under IPW---a concern that also drives mediation analyses with mismeasured mediators~\cite{valeri_vanderweele2013}. All analyze a single DAG topology; ours is the first to characterize how the \emph{same} confusion matrix produces qualitatively different bias across seven topologies.

\paragraph{Text classifiers and LLM annotation.}
\citet{grimmer2022} and \citet{feder_etal2022} provide foundations for text-as-data and causal inference in NLP. \citet{wood_doughty2018} first applied DAG reasoning to classifier error; \citet{teblunthuis2024} gave the most complete binary treatment ($K{=}2$, four DAGs); \citet{lee_wood_doughty2024} extended to categorical LLM classifiers but covered only confounding. On annotation quality, \citet{gilardi2023chatgpt} and \citet{ziems2024can} document the rapid adoption of LLM annotators, \citet{baumann2025} found 31\% incorrect conclusions, \citet{pangakis_wolken_fasching2023} demonstrated that automated annotations require task-specific validation, \citet{cheng_mayya_sedoc2024} propose structured approaches to reduce LLM measurement error, and \citet{kim_garg2025} showed 60\% error correlation across models. \citet{audinet2025} benchmark debiasing; \citet{keith_jensen_oconnor2020} survey the broader landscape. These works either restrict to binary labels, cover a single topology, or diagnose quality without connecting it to causal validity.

\paragraph{Sensitivity analysis and correction methods.}
Our ASV framework draws on E-values~\cite{vanderweele_ding2017}, the robustness value of \citet{cinelli2020making}, and the mediation sensitivity analysis of \citet{imai_keele_yamamoto2010}, but parametrizes annotation error severity rather than omitted variable strength or sequential ignorability violations. The quantitative bias analysis framework of \citet{lash2009} provides a complementary epidemiologic perspective, and \citet{mahajan2006} establishes identification of regression models with misclassified regressors via instrumental variables. The unified measurement-error and missing-data framework of \citet{blackwell_honaker_king2017} similarly motivates treating annotation noise as a first-class object of inference. For correction, PPI++~\cite{angelopoulos2023} and DSL~\cite{egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023} are topology-agnostic; \citet{zrnic_candes2024} extend PPI to gold-free settings, \citet{demirel2024prediction} generalize prediction-powered inference to causal parameters across populations, and \citet{cadei2025} adapt it to treatment-effect targets via a deconfounded risk objective, and \citet{gligoric2025} leverage LLM confidence scores. Our contribution reveals that this agnosticism is costly: correction effectiveness is itself topology-dependent, with matrix-based methods provably ineffective for four topologies. Bayesian measurement error models~\cite{richardson1993bayesian, gustafson2004} and EM imputation offer alternatives that bypass FWL invariance.

